>cause you have to save some fairy trapped in it by a nymph.

Nymphs are not wizards. They are "personifications of the creative and
fostering activities of nature." (Wikipedia) Their only in-game magical
power is supernatural charm - remove that (with a wand of cancellation)
and they are nothing.

Also: NetHack is not very accomodating of Quests. The current Quest
itself involves some shoehorning, especially related to Helms of
Opposite Alignment.

Doublealso: you mean the fairy... is trapped in a sword... which is
trapped in a mirror?

>if you have any ideas how to balance that artifact by adding some penalties it'd be
>great.

First: you can trim even more out of your patch if you use the -b
option with diff (for example: diff -Naurb <foldername>
<otherfoldername>)

Second: you do not, I repeat, NOT need to add another flag to
monflag.h!! If you want to specify "target nymphs" in your artifact
definition, use S_NYMPH in place of M2_NYMPH.

Third: why must it be Yet Another Long Sword? The lesser blades such as
broadsword, short sword and scimitar are decidedly lacking in
artifacts.

Fourth: SPFX_WARN is _supposed_ to be unique to the Tolkien artifacts -
it simulates the "blue glow" that Elven weapons acquire in the presence
of evil.

Fifth: +20 to hit is a bit much. Also, why +d30 versus nymphs instead
of x2? (Specify x2 by making the second number equal 0)

Sixth: altars are NOT _objects_! They are _terrain_! So you should
throw out that autoreadobjnam(action).

Seventh: also, throw out the "create altar" ability. Not only is it
evidently buggy, but it's game-unbalancing.

Eight: remove the lines you added to makemon.c.

Ninth: if you want to make a talking artifact, you should modify
arti_speak(obj) in artifact.c.

Tenth: TEST your patch before releasing it. Patch the code then compile
it then play it in Wizard mode.

And, the name: "Draconyslove" ? ...Actually, it isn't half-bad as far
as sword names go. It'd fit right in with Grayswandir and Orcrist. But
who is "Dracony" ? (And don't say it's you.)



